Q: Smooth horizontal autoscroll while dragging near edges (keep item under finger) A: <p>To make autoscroll smooth with acceleration, change your code with the following suggestions:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Change dragGesture coordinateSpace to <code>.global</code> to make finger movement stable even while the scroll view scrolls.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Don't rely in <code>DragGesture.Value.translation</code> when the view is inside a scrolling container, because that translation is measured in a coordinate space that can move while you scroll, which could cause drift/jumps.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Instead calculate <code>fingerDelta</code> - how far the finger has moved since the drag started in the stable CoordinateSpace <code>.global</code>. With additional properties: Start of drag point - <code>dragStartFingerGlobal</code> , Last or current finger point - <code>lastFingerGlobal</code>. Finger delta would be the difference between those two points.</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>        let fingerDelta = CGSize(
            width: lastFingerGlobal.x - dragStartFingerGlobal.x,
            height: lastFingerGlobal.y - dragStartFingerGlobal.y
        )
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p><code>CADisplayLink</code> is better for autoscroll while dragging. It triggers once per screen refresh, so scrolling updates are smooth. <code>DispatchQueue.asyncAfter</code> is a time-based timer, not a frame-based, that's why scroll step timing could be uneven. With CADisplayLink you get consistent per-frame updates, drag and scroll a bit then update dragged position, render, then repeat. This step should make autoscroll smother.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>1. Add next States to <code>ContentView</code> :</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>@State private var dragStartFingerGlobal: CGPoint? = nil
@State private var lastFingerGlobal: CGPoint = .zero
@State private var dragStartContentOffsetX: CGFloat? = nil
@State private var displayLink: CADisplayLink? = nil
</code></pre>
<p>2. Incorporate helpers:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>/// Converts the finger’s global X position into an X position inside the visible ScrollView viewport (0...sv.bounds.width). 
/// This tells you how close the finger is to the left/right edge to decide autoscroll speed.
private func fingerXInScrollViewBounds(from global: CGPoint, sv: UIScrollView) -&gt; CGFloat {
    let svFrameInWindow = sv.convert(sv.bounds, to: nil)
    return min(max(global.x - svFrameInWindow.minX, 0), sv.bounds.width)
}

/// Recomputes the dragged item’s position so it stays visually under the finger while the scroll view moves
@MainActor
private func updateDraggedItemPinnedToFinger() {
    guard let selectedID = store.selectedItemID,
          let (canvasIndex, itemIndex) = store.indexForItem(id: selectedID),
          let startPos = dragStartItemPosition,
          let startFinger = dragStartFingerGlobal,
          let startOffsetX = dragStartContentOffsetX,
          let sv = scrollView
    else { return }

    let fingerDelta = CGSize(
        width: lastFingerGlobal.x - startFinger.x,
        height: lastFingerGlobal.y - startFinger.y
    )

    // compensate for scroll that happened since drag began
    let scrollDeltaX = sv.contentOffset.x - startOffsetX

    store.canvasesArray[canvasIndex].items[itemIndex].position = CGPoint(
        x: startPos.x + fingerDelta.width + scrollDeltaX,
        y: startPos.y + fingerDelta.height
    )
}
</code></pre>
<p>3. Replace <code>autoScrollIfNeeded</code> method and autoscroll loop with a CADisplayLink that also pins the item each tick:</p>
<pre><code>@MainActor
private func startAutoScrollLoop() {
    guard displayLink == nil else { return }
    let link = CADisplayLink(target: ScrollLinkProxy { [weak store] in
        guard store != nil else { return }
        self.autoScrollTick()
    }, selector: #selector(ScrollLinkProxy.tick))
    link.add(to: .main, forMode: .common)
    displayLink = link
}

private func stopAutoScrollLoop() {
    displayLink?.invalidate()
    displayLink = nil
}

private func autoScrollTick() {
    guard store.isDraggingSelectedItem,
          let sv = scrollView
    else { return }

    // Compute edge acceleration using fingerX inside scroll view bounds
    // Finger X inside the visible ScrollView viewport.
    // 0 = left edge, sv.bounds.width = right edge.
    let fingerXInViewport = fingerXInScrollViewBounds(from: lastFingerGlobal, sv: sv)

    // Start autoscrolling when finger enters this many points from an edge.
    let edgeZoneWidth: CGFloat = 80

    // Scroll step per frame (points per tick). These define min/max speed.
    let minScrollPerFrame: CGFloat = 2
    let maxScrollPerFrame: CGFloat = 18

    let distanceToLeftEdge  = fingerXInViewport
    let distanceToRightEdge = sv.bounds.width - fingerXInViewport

    var scrollStepX: CGFloat = 0

    if distanceToLeftEdge &lt; edgeZoneWidth {
        // closeness: 0 when just inside edge zone, 1 at the very edge
        let closeness = (edgeZoneWidth - distanceToLeftEdge) / edgeZoneWidth
        let eased = closeness * closeness            // accelerate near edge
        let speed = minScrollPerFrame + (maxScrollPerFrame - minScrollPerFrame) * eased
        scrollStepX = -speed                         // negative = scroll left

    } else if distanceToRightEdge &lt; edgeZoneWidth {
        let closeness = (edgeZoneWidth - distanceToRightEdge) / edgeZoneWidth
        let eased = closeness * closeness
        let speed = minScrollPerFrame + (maxScrollPerFrame - minScrollPerFrame) * eased
        scrollStepX = speed                          // positive = scroll right

    } else {
        // Finger not near edges - no autoscroll this frame
        return
    }


    // Apply scroll
    let maxOffsetX = max(0, sv.contentSize.width - sv.bounds.width)
    var offset = sv.contentOffset
    offset.x = min(max(offset.x + scrollStepX, 0), maxOffsetX)
    if offset != sv.contentOffset {
        sv.setContentOffset(offset, animated: false)

        // Immediately recompute dragged item with new contentOffset
        updateDraggedItemPinnedToFinger()
    }
}

// Helper proxy because CADisplayLink target must be ObjC
final class ScrollLinkProxy: NSObject {
    let onTick: () -&gt; Void
    init(_ onTick: @escaping () -&gt; Void) { self.onTick = onTick }
    @objc func tick() { onTick() }
}
</code></pre>
<p>4. Change <code>dragGesture</code> initializing coordinateSpace with <code>.global</code> and add new calculations. Using <code>DragGesture(..., coordinateSpace: .global)</code> helps finger movement to be measured in screen/global coordinates (stable even while the ScrollView autoscrolls):</p>
<pre><code>private var dragGesture: some Gesture {
    DragGesture(minimumDistance: 2, coordinateSpace: .global)
        .onChanged { value in
            guard let selectedID = store.selectedItemID,
                  let (canvasIndex, itemIndex) = store.indexForItem(id: selectedID),
                  let sv = scrollView
            else { return }

            let item = store.canvasesArray[canvasIndex].items[itemIndex]

            if dragStartItemPosition == nil {
                dragStartItemPosition = item.position
                dragStartFingerGlobal = value.location
                dragStartContentOffsetX = sv.contentOffset.x
                store.isDraggingSelectedItem = true
                startAutoScrollLoop()
            }

            lastFingerGlobal = value.location
            updateDraggedItemPinnedToFinger()
        }
        .onEnded { _ in
            stopAutoScrollLoop()
            store.isDraggingSelectedItem = false
            dragStartItemPosition = nil
            dragStartFingerGlobal = nil
            dragStartContentOffsetX = nil
        }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I hope these suggestions will help you with your first problem.</p>
<p>And… Yes, I found what causes your content to disappear.<br />
<code>LazyHStack</code> may unmount views that it thinks are offscreen. During your drag, the selected item is effectively moved “outside” its cell bounds, so the cell can get recycled. <code>HStack</code> keeps all children alive, so it can’t happen.<br />
Change <code>LazyHStack(spacing: 1)</code> inside <code>ScrollView</code> to <code>HStack(spacing: 1)</code><br />
This is the main fix for the disappearing issue. Works good for a small/medium number of canvases. With many, <code>HStack</code> keeps everything alive, so memory use and scrolling performance can degrade.</p>
<p>Small adjustments that I made (edited):<br />
Better mark whole method <code>autoScrollTick()</code> as <code>@MainActor</code> . And inside <code>autoScrollTick()</code> method don't need no more:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>Task { @MainActor in
    updateDraggedItemPinnedToFinger()
}
</code></pre>
<p>and I have replaced it with just <code>updateDraggedItemPinnedToFinger()</code> call.</p>
<p><code>CADisplayLink</code> fires on the main thread, so marking <code>autoScrollTick()</code> as <code>@MainActor</code> means it already runs on the right actor. The item updates immediately without creating a new <code>Task</code> each frame, reducing scheduling overhead and smoothing motion.</p>

Q: How can you create a buffer of the same size as a file? A: <p><a href="https://ziglang.org/download/0.15.1/release-notes.html#Writergate" rel="nofollow noreferrer">0.15.X</a> Update:</p>
<p>Since <code>std.fs.File.readToEndAlloc()</code> is now deprecated, we should instead use <code>std.fs.File.reader()</code> and <code>std.Io.Reader.allocRemaining()</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>const std = @import(&quot;std&quot;);

fn readFile(gpa: std.mem.Allocator, filename: []const u8) ![]u8 {
    const file = try std.fs.cwd().openFile(filename, .{ .mode = .read_only });
    defer file.close();

    // Buffer unneeded since we're using allocRemaining()
    var file_reader = file.reader(&amp;.{});
    return file_reader.interface.allocRemaining(gpa, .unlimited);
}


pub fn main() !void {
    const alloc = std.heap.smp_allocator;
    const filename = &quot;test.txt&quot;;

    const contents = try readFile(alloc, filename);
    defer alloc.free(contents);

    std.debug.print(&quot;{s}&quot;, .{contents});
}
</code></pre>
